{{!
    @template local_byblos/docs/usecase_programme

    Use case: programme-level exit portfolio (capstone) drawing evidence across
    several courses, for demonstration of learning + metacognition.

    Context variables:
    - links (object) Map of topic key => URL.
    - dashurl (string) Portfolio dashboard URL.
    - newpageurl (string) New-page wizard URL.

    @package    local_byblos
    @copyright  2026 South African Theological Seminary
    @license    http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html GNU GPL v3 or later

    Example context (json):
    {
        "dashurl": "#",
        "newpageurl": "#",
        "links": {
            "index": "#", "getting_started": "#", "building_pages": "#",
            "publishing": "#", "collections": "#", "sharing": "#",
            "assessment": "#", "usecase_programme": "#", "usecase_course": "#",
            "usecase_professional": "#"
        }
    }
}}
<div class="byblos-docs-article">

    <h1>{{#str}}docs_topic_usecase_programme, local_byblos{{/str}}</h1>
    <p class="byblos-docs-lead">
        A programme-level exit portfolio (a "capstone") asks you to step back and show what you have
        learned across your whole programme, pulling evidence from several courses, and to reflect on
        <em>how</em> you have grown. Byblos is well suited to this because artefacts are not locked to a
        single course, and a collection lets you present many strands of evidence as one coherent
        mini-site.
    </p>

    <div class="byblos-docs-note">
        <strong>The shape we'll build.</strong> An introductory "About this portfolio" home page that
        frames your learning journey, then one page per programme outcome (or theme), each weaving
        together evidence from different courses with your own reflection. All gathered into a single
        collection you share with your assessor.
    </div>

    <h2>Example</h2>
    <p>
        <em>Thandi is finishing a three-year degree. Her programme has five graduate outcomes. She
        builds a home page introducing herself and her growth, then five outcome pages, each citing an
        assignment from one course, a badge from another, and a reflection tying them to that outcome.
        She gathers the six pages into one collection and shares it with her programme coordinator.</em>
    </p>

    <h2>Step 1: Gather your evidence as artefacts</h2>
    <ol class="byblos-docs-steps">
        <li>Open the <strong>{{#str}}nav_artefacts, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> tab on your dashboard.</li>
        <li>Check what is already there, earned <strong>badges</strong> and <strong>course completions</strong> are imported automatically if your site enables it.</li>
        <li>Add anything else: upload key assignments or files, add links, and write short <strong>reflections</strong>. Because artefacts span courses, you can pull a single library of evidence from across the whole programme.</li>
    </ol>

    <figure class="byblos-docs-figure">
        <div class="byblos-docs-shot" role="img" aria-label="The Artefacts tab with mixed evidence">
            <i class="fa fa-camera" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            <span>Screenshot: the Artefacts tab showing a mix of evidence types, an uploaded essay, a course-completion item, a badge, and a reflection, drawn from different courses.</span>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Figure 1: A cross-course evidence library.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Step 2: Build one page per outcome</h2>
    <ol class="byblos-docs-steps">
        <li>Create a new page for each programme outcome, the <strong>Learning Journey</strong> template suits reflective, evidence-led pages (see <a href="{{{links.building_pages}}}">Building pages</a>).</li>
        <li>On each page, state the outcome, then add evidence sections, Artefacts, Badges, Completions, a Files list, an image Gallery, drawing from <em>different</em> courses.</li>
        <li>After each piece of evidence, add a <strong>Text</strong> section of reflection: what it shows, what you learned, what you would do differently. This reflection is what turns evidence into demonstration of learning.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="byblos-docs-tip">
        <strong>Make the metacognition visible.</strong> Assessors of capstone portfolios are looking for
        self-awareness, not just artefacts. Use a Pull quote for a guiding insight, a Timeline to show how
        your thinking changed over the programme, and reflective Text sections that name your growth
        explicitly.
    </div>

    <h2>Step 3: Gather the pages into a collection</h2>
    <ol class="byblos-docs-steps">
        <li>Create a collection, for example "BA Exit Portfolio" (see <a href="{{{links.collections}}}">Collections</a>).</li>
        <li>Add your outcome pages plus an introductory page.</li>
        <li>Make the introduction the <strong>{{#str}}collection_home_badge, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> with <strong>{{#str}}collection_make_home, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>, and order the outcome pages logically.</li>
        <li>Optionally add a <strong>Page navigation</strong> section to the home page so readers can jump straight to any outcome.</li>
    </ol>

    <figure class="byblos-docs-figure">
        <div class="byblos-docs-shot" role="img" aria-label="The exit-portfolio collection">
            <i class="fa fa-camera" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            <span>Screenshot: the collection view for an exit portfolio, an "About this portfolio" home page badged as the home page, followed by five outcome pages in order.</span>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Figure 2: The capstone as one navigable collection.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Step 4: Publish and hand it over</h2>
    <ol class="byblos-docs-steps">
        <li><a href="{{{links.publishing}}}">Publish</a> each page in the collection.</li>
        <li><a href="{{{links.sharing}}}">Share</a> the collection with your assessor, by name (<strong>{{#str}}share_user, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>) or with the programme's review course (<strong>{{#str}}share_course, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>). The whole mini-site travels as one share.</li>
        <li>If your programme assesses the capstone through a Moodle assignment, submit the <em>collection</em> instead, choose "Snapshot on submit" so your exit record is frozen at hand-in. See <a href="{{{links.assessment}}}">Submitting for assessment</a>.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="byblos-docs-note">
        <strong>Reuse, don't duplicate.</strong> The same artefact (say, a capstone project file) can
        appear on more than one outcome page. Build your evidence once and reference it wherever it is
        relevant.
    </div>
</div>
